From 3e754507d2978b16db63da2300bffb679da5fdb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:58:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: bound the per-frame audio-ring pump by iteration count,
 not just ms

Real-hardware measurement on the port's actual minimum-target CPU
(486DX2-50, PIT-timer fallback -- confirmed via "audio IRQ timer: 8253
PIT counter 0" in SDLDBG.LOG, no RDTSC available) showed the prior
150ms-target-depth revision still catastrophic: 2.09 fps measured (a
15fps KPI), while the renderer's own instrumentation shows its per-
flush cost (~51ms average) only explains a ceiling around ~19fps. The
~427ms/frame gap points at SDL_DOSAudioPump() itself: on hardware this
slow, each individual call costs real, non-trivial wall-clock time
(mixing/DMA-write work that scales with CPU speed, not with how much
audio-time each call produces) -- so reaching even the smaller 150ms
target still means many expensive calls. The 150ms-target fix reduced
total ms-of-audio needing catch-up (which helped on the faster Pentium
OverDrive 83 test rig), but didn't address that cost-per-call, not
call-count-to-reach-a-target, dominates on genuinely slow hardware.

Added a hard cap on how many SDL_DOSAudioPump() calls this loop makes
per frame (4), independent of whether the 150ms target was reached.
This directly bounds worst-case per-frame pump cost regardless of CPU
speed, at the cost of some ring-fill margin (more underrun risk on
very slow hardware) -- an explicit tradeoff toward keeping the game
itself playable first: a 2fps game is a worse failure than choppy
audio, and choppy/dragging audio on real 486DX2-50 hardware (user-
confirmed, live) was the visible symptom of this same underlying
per-call cost problem, not a separate one.

Note for whoever reads SDL_DOSAudioPump's own >32-consecutive-call
"cap tripped" counter after this change: since that counter only
resets on an actual false return from the function (ring full, cap
tripped, or no device open) -- not merely on this call site's own loop
exiting early at 4 -- the counter can still accumulate to 32 and trip
*across* multiple frames (e.g. 8 frames of 4 successful calls each)
even though no single frame ever calls it more than 4 times. Cap-trip
*count* alone no longer indicates per-frame cost after this change;
per-frame call count is now the more meaningful signal, and it's
directly bounded to 4 by this patch regardless of what the cap-trip
log shows.

Verified under DOSBox-X: still converges to fully non-silent
(silence-histo noisy_n reaching 100/100), same as before this change.
Real-hardware re-verification (fps AND audio quality) on the 486DX2-50
is the next step -- this is an experimental, conservative mitigation
prioritizing frame rate, not a final answer to the underlying cost
driver.
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index c55519c..9c9561b 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -1030,15 +1030,41 @@ char playGame() {
         // under a full ring (doskutsu's own production ring is ~372 ms)
         // -- is expected to be enough of a safety margin without
         // pumping nearly as hard every frame.
+        //
+        // Real-hardware measurement on the port's actual minimum-target
+        // CPU (486DX2-50, PIT-timer fallback -- no RDTSC) showed the
+        // 150ms-target version above still catastrophic: 2.09 fps
+        // measured (vs. a 15fps KPI), while the renderer's own per-
+        // flush cost (~51ms average) only explains a ceiling around
+        // ~19fps. The gap is SDL_DOSAudioPump() itself: on hardware
+        // this slow, each individual call is expensive in real
+        // wall-clock terms (mixing/DMA-write work that scales with CPU
+        // speed, not with how much audio-time each call produces), so
+        // reaching even a *smaller* ms-of-audio target still means many
+        // expensive calls -- the 150ms-target fix (see above) reduced
+        // TOTAL ms needing catch-up, which helped on the faster Pentium
+        // OverDrive 83 test rig, but didn't address the fact that the
+        // COST PER CALL is what actually dominates on genuinely slow
+        // hardware. Capping the iteration COUNT directly, not just the
+        // ms target, bounds worst-case per-frame pump cost regardless
+        // of CPU speed. 4 is a conservative starting point, trading
+        // some audio ring-fill margin (more underrun risk on very slow
+        // hardware) for keeping the game itself playable -- a 2fps game
+        // is a worse failure than choppy audio. Real-hardware
+        // re-verification pending.
         {
             const int pumpTargetMs = 150;
+            const int pumpMaxIters = 4;
             int pumpCapacityMs = SDL_DOSAudioRingCapacityMs();
             int pumpClampedTargetMs = pumpTargetMs;
             if( pumpCapacityMs > 0 && pumpClampedTargetMs > pumpCapacityMs ) {
                 pumpClampedTargetMs = pumpCapacityMs;
                 }
-            while( SDL_DOSAudioRingFillMs() < pumpClampedTargetMs &&
+            int pumpIters = 0;
+            while( pumpIters < pumpMaxIters &&
+                   SDL_DOSAudioRingFillMs() < pumpClampedTargetMs &&
                    SDL_DOSAudioPump() ) {
+                pumpIters ++;
                 }
             }
 #endif
-- 
